<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Maritime Southeast Asia</p><h1 class="st-title">Kingdom of Buleleng</h1><p class="st-meta">1660 CE – 1849 CE (approx.) · capital Singaraja</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">North Balinese kingdom founded by the warrior Panji Sakti, whose defeat in 1849 began the Dutch conquest of Bali.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Buleleng was founded around 1660 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gusti-panji-sakti">Gusti Panji Sakti</a>, a noble of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-gelgel">Gelgel</a> court who built a power base on the north coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bali">Bali</a>. He made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/singaraja">Singaraja</a> his capital and extended his rule across the strait to <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/blambangan-kingdom">Blambangan</a> in eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/java">Java</a> in 1697, making Buleleng briefly the leading Balinese state.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The kingdom looked outward through its north-coast harbours, trading <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a> and slaves with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bugis">Bugis</a>, Chinese and European merchants. It was ruled by a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> raja and his noble kin, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahmins">Brahmana</a> priests at court. In the 18th century it passed for a time under the control of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-karangasem">Kingdom of Karangasem</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Disputes over the plunder of shipwrecks led the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> to attack in 1846 and 1848, when the patih <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gusti-ketut-jelantik">Gusti Ketut Jelantik</a> defeated them at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-jagaraga">Jagaraga</a>. A third expedition in 1849 conquered Buleleng, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/singaraja">Singaraja</a> became the Dutch administrative centre for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bali">Bali</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lombok">Lombok</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="kingdom, 1660 CE – 1849 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M22 72 V40 L36 54 L50 28 L64 54 L78 40 V72 Z"/><path d="M22 80 H78"/><circle cx="50" cy="24" r="3"/><circle cx="22" cy="36" r="2.5"/><circle cx="78" cy="36" r="2.5"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">kingdom</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Maritime Southeast Asia</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:92.74%;width:3.76%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1660 CE – 1849 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Singaraja</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Gusti Panji Sakti</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Balinese Hinduism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Balinese</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/indonesia">Indonesia</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Buleleng</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1754&amp;focus=kingdom-of-buleleng" aria-label="Kingdom of Buleleng on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7861 2481 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Kingdom of Buleleng lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M8181 2737L8195 2749M8181 2737L7959 2663M8181 2737L8168 2746"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M8195 2749h0M7959 2663h0M8168 2746h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M8181 2737h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M8181 2737h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Singaraja. <a href="/atlas?year=1754&amp;focus=kingdom-of-buleleng">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

